From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NotAlexNoyle <alexnoyle@icloud.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 02:57:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] gpu compositor: the card's scroll applied in the compositor;
 frame-sized surfaces; band frames on their own surface

T-502 (2026-10-18). Without APZ a scroll was a main-thread scroll: every step repainted the
newly exposed strip and re-uploaded the content layer. The daemon now sends the card's scroll
(jihad_offscreen_set_scroll) and AsyncCompositionManager applies it as an async transform on the
layer carrying the root content metrics, the way APZ would, with AlignFixedAndStickyLayers keeping
fixed and sticky layers still. For that, without APZ: root scroll metadata and the scrollport as a
scroll clip for a root scroll frame with a displayport (nsDisplayList, nsGfxScrollFrame), the
displayport set from layout (jihad_layout_set_displayport), and no intermediate surface for a
container under an axis-aligned scale+translate (Layers.cpp).

The Adreno 220 driver bins an FBO render by the current EGL surface's size: a 942-row frame in a
1400-row pbuffer lost its bottom 458 GL rows in some composites. The context's pbuffer is made the
frame's size (GLContextEGL::JihadUsePBufferOfSize) and the window targets with it. Scroll-only
frames (no composite request since the last composite) compose their bands stacked on their own
768x256 pbuffer and FBO, which stays current between band frames (the switch into it costs
11-13 ms); the rows kept are moved while the GPU works; one read takes the stack. A done-fd
(jihad_offscreen_set_done_fd) wakes the daemon the moment a job finishes. Nothing touches a job
after its hand-off.
